From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Builder <builder@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/meson: size the console framebuffer from the active mode

fbdev_sizes is a fixed device tree property, so the console framebuffer
never follows the mode the display is actually driven at. When the two
disagree, fbcon writes scanlines of one width into a plane scanned out at
another: the picture breaks into repeated strips and the OSD scaler blurs
what is left of it. The original symptom on this board was a device tree
asking for 1280x720 while boot.ini drove the panel at 1080p60.

Prefer the mode the vout server has resolved, and fall back to the device
tree when no mode has been resolved -- which is also what happens when
CONFIG_AMLOGIC_VOUT_SERVE is absent and get_current_vinfo() is the inline
stub returning NULL.

A note on what this is worth today, so it is not mistaken for dead code:
the board device tree was separately raised to
fbdev_sizes = <1920 1080 1920 2160 32> in the same change that first added
this patch, so for the default boot.ini the device tree and the active
mode already agree and this patch changes nothing. It earns its place by
keeping them in agreement when they would otherwise diverge -- a different
outputmode in boot.ini, a sink that forces a different mode, or a device
tree that is not updated in lockstep -- rather than by fixing the one
mismatch that has already been fixed by hand. The mismatch it guards
against is silent: it produces a corrupt picture, not an error.

REBASE NOTE (6.12) -- what moved, and what was re-checked.

Only the include hunk needed real work. The other two hunks moved by 192
lines but their surroundings are unchanged, so they were regenerated
against the new line numbers with no change in content.

The include hunk failed because the block it anchored on has been
rewritten: <drm/drm_blend.h> and <linux/fb.h> were added, and the
<linux/sysrq.h> line the old hunk used as trailing context is gone,
replaced by a conditional <trace/events/meson_atrace.h> block guarded by
CONFIG_AMLOGIC_DEBUG_ATRACE. The new hunk anchors on
<linux/amlogic/ion.h> instead and inserts the same single include
immediately after it, so the outcome is identical -- only the context
lines changed.

Facts re-confirmed against 6.12, since the whole patch depends on them:

  - common_drivers/drivers/drm/meson_fbdev.c still exists at that path,
    and is still built (CONFIG_AMLOGIC_DRM=m selects the aml_drm module,
    CONFIG_AMLOGIC_DRM_EMULATE_FBDEV=y adds meson_fbdev.o to it).
  - struct vinfo_s still carries name, width and height, as char * and two
    u32 respectively.
  - get_current_vinfo() is still declared in
    <linux/amlogic/media/vout/vout_notify.h>, still behind
    CONFIG_AMLOGIC_VOUT_SERVE, and the #else branch is still a
    static inline returning NULL. So the fallback path is intact.
  - The function being patched is still am_meson_fbdev_parse_config(), and
    it is still called from am_meson_drm_fbdev_init(). private->ui_config
    still has ui_w, ui_h, fb_w and fb_h.
  - The sentinel mode names the guard rejects still exist and are still
    spelled the same way: "invalid" in vout_func.c and both "null" and
    "invalid" in vout_null_server.c.

On the ordering guarantee:

The previous version of this header justified the ordering with boot log
timestamps (vout probe at 2.548s, am_meson_drm_fbdev_init at 2.651s).
Those were measured on the previous kernel and have deliberately not been
carried over, because no board is reachable from here and they cannot be
re-measured on 6.12. They are replaced with a structural argument, which
is checkable from the source:

get_current_vinfo() is EXPORT_SYMBOL()ed from vout_notify.c, which is
linked into aml_media. meson_fbdev.c is linked into aml_drm. aml_drm
therefore has a module dependency on aml_media, so aml_media is loaded --
and its init has run, registering the vout platform driver and letting the
vout device probe -- before any code in aml_drm executes. That is a
stronger statement than the timestamps were, and it does not depend on
timing.

It is worth being explicit that this patch cannot make the current
behaviour worse even if that argument is wrong. Every path that does not
yield a resolved mode -- get_current_vinfo() returning NULL, a zero width
or height, a NULL name, or one of the "null"/"invalid" placeholder modes
-- falls through and leaves the device tree values exactly as they are
today. The patch only ever replaces the device tree size with a mode the
vout server has actually resolved.

Judgement call: fb_h is set to twice the mode height, keeping the double
buffering the device tree value encoded (fbdev_sizes carries fb_h as twice
ui_h). fb_w is set to the mode width with no multiplier, for the same
reason. This mirrors what the device tree was expressing rather than
inventing a new allocation policy, but it does mean the console
framebuffer allocation grows when the active mode is larger than the
device tree mode -- 1080p double buffered at 32bpp is about 16MiB against
about 7MiB for 720p. That is a deliberate consequence and not a leak.

Not verified: no display, HDMI or console behaviour has been observed, and
none can be from here. The reasoning above is from the 6.12 source only.
---
--- a/common_drivers/drivers/drm/meson_fbdev.c
+++ b/common_drivers/drivers/drm/meson_fbdev.c
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include <linux/dma-buf.h>
 #include <linux/fb.h>
 #include <linux/amlogic/ion.h>
+#include <linux/amlogic/media/vout/vout_notify.h>
 #if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_AMLOGIC_DEBUG_ATRACE)
 #define KERNEL_ATRACE_TAG KERNEL_ATRACE_TAG_DRM
 #include <trace/events/meson_atrace.h>
@@ -972,6 +973,7 @@
 {
 	struct meson_drm *private = dev->dev_private;
 	struct meson_vpu_pipeline *pipeline = private->pipeline;
+	const struct vinfo_s *vinfo;
 	u32 sizes[5], overlay_sizes[5];
 	int ret, tmp, i;
 
@@ -998,6 +1000,36 @@
 		private->ui_config.overlay_fb_h = overlay_sizes[3];
 		private->ui_config.overlay_fb_bpp = overlay_sizes[4];
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * fbdev_sizes is a fixed value in the device tree, so the console
+	 * framebuffer does not follow the mode the display is actually driven
+	 * at. When the two disagree, fbcon writes scanlines of one width into a
+	 * plane scanned out at another: the image breaks into repeated strips
+	 * and whatever the OSD scaler makes of it is blurred.
+	 *
+	 * The vout server has already resolved the boot mode by the time fbdev
+	 * is set up, so prefer what is really being output and leave the device
+	 * tree value as the fallback for when no mode is known yet.
+	 */
+	vinfo = get_current_vinfo();
+	if (vinfo && vinfo->width && vinfo->height && vinfo->name &&
+	    strcmp(vinfo->name, "invalid") != 0 &&
+	    strcmp(vinfo->name, "null") != 0) {
+		if (private->ui_config.ui_w != vinfo->width ||
+		    private->ui_config.ui_h != vinfo->height)
+			DRM_INFO("fbdev: following vout mode %s (%ux%u), device tree said %ux%u\n",
+				 vinfo->name, vinfo->width, vinfo->height,
+				 private->ui_config.ui_w,
+				 private->ui_config.ui_h);
+
+		private->ui_config.ui_w = vinfo->width;
+		private->ui_config.ui_h = vinfo->height;
+		private->ui_config.fb_w = vinfo->width;
+		/* double buffered */
+		private->ui_config.fb_h = vinfo->height * 2;
+	}
+
 	/*initial fbdev zorder*/
 	for (i = 0; i < MESON_MAX_OSD; i++)
 		private->fbdev_zorder[i] = i;
